This reaction is a classic, highly efficient nucleophilic addition. The nitrogen atom of the amine attacks the electrophilic carbon of the isocyanate, followed by a proton transfer (likely intermolecularly or assisted by the base/solvent) to yield the urea product. The reaction is generally fast, exothermic, and high-yielding. The proposed conditions, using triethylamine (TEA) as a base in dichloromethane (DCM), are standard for this transformation.

